;;; vscode-extension.cnj
;;; The VS Code extension (VSIX) — companion UI over the shared core.
;;; References the project anchor for charter, assets, and assumptions.
;;; Models: the extension's user-facing features (Section V) and its module
;;; decomposition + bundling strategy (Section VI).

(charter "Conjurer VS Code Extension"
  :version  "0.1.0"
  :created  "2026-06-05"
  :updated  "2026-10-14"
  :domain   "A VS Code extension giving .cnj and Conjurer authors inline,
             editor-native access to the spec — CodeLens actions, hover docs, and
             commands — built on the same @conjurer/core as the MCP server."
  :language :en
  :goals [
    "Surface spec content where the author already is: hovering a symbol shows its
     synopsis; a CodeLens above a charter offers validate-and-summarise; commands
     expose lookup and section retrieval — all without leaving the editor."
    "Support Explorer right-click materialisation on any selected folder that
     contains .cnj design inputs (folder name is unconstrained). The command must
     tolerate references outside the selected folder, and must show explicit
     unresolved/non-readable references before materialisation output is emitted."
    "Author the extension as modular TypeScript bundled to a single
     dist/extension.js, fixing the prior single-source-file extension.js."]
  :audience {:primary :senior-engineer}
  :decisions [
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "Source is modular TypeScript under the TSF++ limit; esbuild bundles
                it to dist/extension.js. The single-file output is a build artefact.
                This is the explicit fix for the prior 'one giant extension.js'."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "The extension depends on @conjurer/core directly for v1 (in-process
                lookup/parse/validate), NOT by spawning the MCP server. Rationale:
                simpler, no subprocess lifecycle, and the core is already the shared
                home of the logic. Talking MCP to a spawned server is a later option
                if the extension ever needs tool parity with external agents."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "Materialise Folder is exposed through Explorer context menu for
                any folder selection, not by fixed folder name. Default generated
                package identity is conjurer-mcp, with override option in command
                UI. Unresolved/non-readable references are displayed explicitly in
                preflight diagnostics rather than silently ignored."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "No extension/server split-brain: folder preflight uses the shared
                core preflight-resolver via core-bridge. If MCP transport parity
                is added later, server materialise_preflight remains a thin wrapper
                over the same resolver and report shape."}
    {:id :validate-on-save-2026-06-10
     :date "2026-06-10"
     :decision "validate-on-save: validate the active .cnj on every save via
                onDidSaveTextDocument; violations surface as squiggles and Problems
                panel entries via vscode.DiagnosticCollection. No debounce needed
                at the save event boundary. Resolves :open question."}
    {:id :codelens-candidate-score-2026-06-10
     :date "2026-06-10"
     :decision "codelens-candidate-score: emit a 'score: N · status' CodeLens on
                every f/candidate opening line. Format chosen: 'score: 33 · planned'.
                Clicking the lens shows an information message with id, score, and status
                via conjurer.showCandidateDetails command. Parsing is lazy — toCnjModel
                is only called when at least one f/candidate line is found in the file."}
    {:id :foresight-health-statusbar-2026-06-10
     :date "2026-06-10"
     :decision "foresight-health-statusbar: trigger on onDidChangeActiveTextEditor
                (file-open). No FileSystemWatcher needed for v1 — status reflects the
                saved state of the workspace at the point the file is opened. Clicking
                the status bar item opens a QuickPick listing all health issues."}
    {:date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "VSIX packaging metadata: license is MIT; package.json carries 'license': 'MIT' and
                a 'repository' field pointing to the project's source repository. A LICENSE file
                is required at the extension package root for vsce to accept the build without warnings.
                Publisher remains 'conjurer' for local VSIX installs; Marketplace publication deferred
                (resolves the :open question — local-only for v1)."}
    {:id :candidate-lifecycle-gutter-2026-06-12
     :date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "candidate-lifecycle-gutter: inline SVG data-URI circles in the gutter per f/candidate
                opening line (orange=:proposed, blue=:planned, green=:hoisted, grey=:abandoned).
                Icon representation decided as inline SVG via vscode.Uri.parse(data:...) — no asset
                files required. New providers/candidate-gutter.ts, registered on onDidChangeActiveTextEditor
                and onDidSaveTextDocument."}
    {:id :charter-ref-lens-2026-06-12
     :date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "charter-ref-lens: a CodeLens on the charter line showing 'N/N refs resolved' (green) or
                'X broken reference(s)' (amber). Zero-references case: show nothing — suppress the lens
                entirely for single-file projects. Implemented inline in providers/codelens.ts."}
    {:id :disk-lint-decorations-2026-06-12
     :date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "disk-lint-decorations: gutter ✓/✗ decoration per target block indicating whether all
                declared :modules exist on disk. Update cadence: on-save with 500 ms debounce via
                onDidSaveTextDocument. New providers/disk-lint.ts, registered in extension.ts."}
    {:id :codelens-candidate-actions-2026-06-12
     :date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "codelens-candidate-actions: Reassess (📊) and Hoist (⬆) buttons on f/candidate CodeLens.
                Reassess available for :proposed and :planned; Hoist appears only when :planned and
                score ≥ 28. f/reassess insertion contract: standalone block appended after the closing
                paren of the f/candidate form. Input via sequential showInputBox calls. New
                commands/reassess.ts registers conjurer.reassessCandidate and conjurer.hoistCandidate."}
    {:date "2026-06-10"
     :decision "hoisted-form-folding: f/hoist forms and f/candidate forms with
                :status :hoisted are collapsed by default on first open of a .cnj file.
                Implementation: FoldingRangeProvider (providers/folding.ts) declares
                the ranges; onDidChangeActiveTextEditor + a per-session Set fires
                editor.fold on first activation only — tab-switches do not re-collapse
                forms the user has manually expanded. computeHoistFoldRanges is pure
                and uses a boundary scan (top-level form starts) so body content
                (:status :hoisted) can be checked without a full parse."}
    {:id :prompt-target-setting
     :date "2026-10-14"
     :decision "The AI-assisted commands hand their prompt to the chat named by the setting
                conjurer.promptTarget: vscodeChat (the built-in chat view, which is GitHub
                Copilot Chat; the default, so existing behaviour is unchanged), claudeCode or
                clipboard. claudeCode runs claude-vscode.editor.open(sessionId, initialPrompt),
                which opens a new Claude Code tab with the prompt in its input box, not sent,
                so the practitioner reviews it first. That is the Claude Code extension's own
                call signature, read from version 2.1.299, not a published API; when the
                command fails the prompt goes to the clipboard with a note saying why. The
                choice of command is pure (commands/chat-handoff.ts) and unit-tested; the
                extension still calls no model itself."}
    {:id :codelens-tooltips
     :date "2026-10-14"
     :decision "Every CodeLens carries a one-line tooltip saying what clicking it does; lenses
                that hand a prompt to a chat start with 'AI:'. The tooltip is a required field
                of makeLens, so a new lens cannot be added without one."}
    {:id :decide-candidate
     :date "2026-10-14"
     :decision "A '⚖ Decide' CodeLens on :proposed candidates hands the chat a prompt that must
                end in a verdict: plan (:status :planned), keep (no status change, but only with
                a sharpened, checkable condition) or abandon (:status :abandoned); 'needs more
                thought' is not an outcome. The prompt asks for a substantive evaluation (the
                problem and its evidence, the cost of never doing it, fit with :goals, overlap
                with decisions, built code and the other active candidates, the state of the
                :conditions), then has the model ask the practitioner at most four questions
                that would change the verdict, and wait. The verdict is recorded as one dated
                f/reassess appended after the candidate, the same insertion contract as
                :codelens-candidate-actions-2026-06-12. The spec's lifecycle is kept as it is:
                postponing is keep, discarding is abandon. No new status is invented here.
                The builder is pure (commands/decide-prompt.ts) and takes today's date as a
                parameter."}
    {:id :candidate-score-lens-effective
     :date "2026-10-14"
     :decision "The candidate score lens and the Reassess defaults show the effective scores
                (each dimension from its latest reassessment, foresight.md · f/reassess), not
                the scores as first written, so a reassessment moves the badge."}
    {:id         :reassess-into-history
     :date       "2026-10-14"
     :supersedes :codelens-candidate-actions-2026-06-12
     :decision   "f/reassess insertion contract reversed: a reassessment is placed inside the
                  candidate's :history vector, appended as its last entry, or as a new
                  :history [...] on the line after :scores when the candidate has none. It is
                  no longer a standalone block after the closing paren. One form per candidate
                  folds as one unit and matches how :search_decisions was already written; the
                  derived status and effective scores read the same either way. Applies to the
                  Reassess button (commands/reassess.ts, placement in the pure
                  commands/history-insert.ts) and to the Decide prompt
                  (commands/decide-prompt.ts), so :decide-candidate's reference to the old
                  contract now means this one. The rest of :codelens-candidate-actions-2026-06-12
                  stands; f/hoist is still a standalone block. The button's f/reassess now
                  follows foresight.md: :scores carries only the dimensions that changed (and
                  is omitted when none did), and the non-spec :total field is dropped.
                  The 2026-06-12 VSIX entry in
                  conjurer-mcp.cnj, which states the old contract in prose, is left as written."}
    {:id       :fold-closed-candidates
     :date     "2026-10-14"
     :decision "Default folding extends from hoisted to all closed candidates: an f/candidate
                is collapsed on first open when its current status is :hoisted or :abandoned.
                The status is the core model's derived one (toCnjModel: hoisted once an f/hoist
                names it, otherwise the latest :status in its reassessments, nested or
                standalone), the same the CodeLens shows; the candidate's own :status is never
                edited, so it cannot be read off the form. While the file does not parse, the
                fold falls back to the line scan (f/hoist names and :status :hoisted). This
                extends the undated-id 2026-06-10 hoisted-form-folding entry, which carries no
                :id and so is named here in prose."}]
  :open []
  :outputs {
    :vscode-extension (target :typescript :standard :tsf-plus-plus)}
  :references ["conjurer-mcp.cnj"]
  :tags [:vscode :extension :typescript :developer-experience])

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section V — Domain work: extension features
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(spell hover-symbol
  :feature     "Hover documentation"
  :purpose     "Hovering a Conjurer symbol in a .cnj or grimoire shows its synopsis,
                signature, and key-params — pulled from the core, no file opened"
  :trigger     "editor hover over a recognised symbol token"
  :backed-by   :symbol-index
  :shows       [:synopsis :signature :key-params]
  :determinism :pure)

(spell codelens-charter
  :feature     "Charter CodeLens"
  :purpose     "A CodeLens above a (charter ...) form offering inline actions:
                validate this .cnj, summarise the charter, list open questions"
  :trigger     "a charter form detected at the top of a .cnj"
  :actions     ["Validate .cnj" "Summarise charter" "Show open questions"]
  :backed-by   :validation   ;; and cnj-model for the summary
  :determinism :pure)

(spell codelens-symbol
  :feature     "Symbol CodeLens"
  :purpose     "A CodeLens above a recognised construct invocation offering
                'Show examples' and 'Show rationale', fetching only that section"
  :trigger     "a known symbol invocation in a .cnj"
  :actions     ["Show examples" "Show rationale" "Open grimoire section"]
  :backed-by   :spec-reader
  :determinism :pure)

(spell command-lookup
  :feature     "Command: Conjurer — Look up symbol"
  :purpose     "Command-palette entry that prompts for a symbol and shows its full
                record in a panel; the editor-native twin of lookup_symbol"
  :trigger     "command palette"
  :backed-by   :symbol-index
  :determinism :pure)

(spell command-validate
  :feature     "Command: Conjurer — Validate current .cnj"
  :purpose     "Validate the active .cnj and surface violations as editor
                diagnostics (squiggles + Problems panel)"
  :trigger     "command palette or on-save (see :open)"
  :backed-by   :validation
  :produces    [:diagnostics]
  :determinism :pure)

(spell command-materialise-folder
  :feature     "Command: Conjurer — Materialise folder"
  :purpose     "Explorer right-click command over a selected folder containing
                .cnj design inputs. Runs deterministic preflight, then materialises
                outputs using local IDE semantic generation policy."
  :trigger     "Explorer context menu on folder"
  :accepts     {:folder       "selected folder path (name unconstrained)"
                :project_name "optional string — defaults to conjurer-mcp"}
  :produces    {:deterministic [:preflight-report :resolved-refs :unresolved-refs]
                :semantic      [:materialisation-plan :workspace-files]}
  :preflight   {:must-report [:resolved-outside-folder :unresolved :non-readable]
                :blocks-if   [:unresolved-critical]}
  :backed-by   :preflight-resolver
  :then        :claude-materialise
  :determinism :hybrid)

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section VI — Module decomposition and bundling (TSF++: ≤ 400 lines/module)
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(target :vscode-extension
  :language  :typescript
  :framework :vscode-extension-api
  :standard  :tsf-plus-plus
  :from      :shared-core
  :via       :direct
  :produces  [:vsix :bundled-extension]
  :bundling  {:tool :esbuild
      :entry "packages/vscode-extension/src/extension.ts"
      :output "packages/vscode-extension/dist/extension.js"
              :note "Single-file output is a BUILD ARTEFACT. Source stays modular.
                     external: ['vscode']; platform: 'node'; format: 'cjs'."}
  :modules [
    {:module "extension.ts"
     :responsibility "activate()/deactivate() entry: register providers and
                      commands, then nothing else. Wiring only."
     :exports ["activate" "deactivate"]
     :est-lines 90}
    {:module "providers/hover.ts"
     :responsibility "HoverProvider — resolve the token under the cursor to a
                      symbol record via core, render markdown."
     :exports ["ConjurerHoverProvider"]
     :est-lines 130}
    {:module "providers/codelens.ts"
     :responsibility "CodeLensProvider — detect charter and symbol forms, emit the
                      lenses, their tooltips and their command bindings."
     :exports ["ConjurerCodeLensProvider" "mkCodeLensProvider" "codeLensesFor"]
     :est-lines 290}
    {:module "commands/lookup.ts"
     :responsibility "Look-up and section commands; render results in a webview or
                      output channel."
     :exports ["registerLookupCommands"]
     :est-lines 160}
    {:module "commands/validate.ts"
     :responsibility "Validate command + on-save hook; map core Violations to
                      vscode.Diagnostic."
     :exports ["registerValidateCommands"]
     :est-lines 150}
    {:module "commands/materialise-folder.ts"
     :responsibility "Explorer context-menu command for folder materialisation:
              invoke deterministic preflight, show unresolved diagnostics,
              and hand ready plans to semantic generation stage."
     :exports ["registerMaterialiseFolderCommand"]
     :est-lines 220}
    {:module "core-bridge.ts"
     :responsibility "Thin adapter wrapping @conjurer/core for the extension:
                      one place that constructs the SpecReader/SymbolIndex and
                      hands them to providers/commands."
     :exports ["createCoreBridge" "CoreBridge"]
     :est-lines 110}
    {:module "providers/status-bar.ts"
     :responsibility "Foresight health status bar item: computeHealthIssues (pure, testable)
                      over WorkspaceSnapshot + VS Code wiring for onDidChangeActiveTextEditor
                      and the conjurer.showForesightHealth command."
     :exports ["computeHealthIssues" "registerForesightStatusBar" "HealthIssue" "HealthIssueKind"]
     :est-lines 120}
    {:module "providers/folding.ts"
     :responsibility "FoldingRangeProvider + foldHoistFormsOnOpen: collapse f/hoist forms
                      and f/candidate :status :hoisted forms by default on first file open.
                      computeHoistFoldRanges is pure (boundary scan, no vscode dependency)."
     :exports ["computeHoistFoldRanges" "createFoldingProvider" "foldHoistFormsOnOpen" "FoldRange"]
     :est-lines 70}
    {:module "commands/refine.ts"
     :responsibility "VS Code adapter for the AI-assisted commands (refineForm,
                      resolveOpenQuestions, critiqueCandidate, argueCandidate,
                      decideCandidate, refreshGoals): read the form or charter, build
                      the prompt and hand it to chat-handoff. Calls no model itself."
     :exports ["registerRefineCommands"]
     :est-lines 160}
    {:module "commands/chat-handoff.ts"
     :responsibility "Hand a prompt to the chat chosen in conjurer.promptTarget (VS Code
                      chat, Claude Code or the clipboard), falling back to the clipboard
                      when that chat cannot be opened. Choosing the command is pure."
     :exports ["openInChat" "parsePromptTarget" "chatCommandFor" "PromptTarget"
               "PROMPT_TARGETS" "DEFAULT_PROMPT_TARGET" "ChatCommand"]
     :est-lines 70}
    {:module "commands/refine-prompts.ts"
     :responsibility "Pure prompt builders for the refine commands, split out of
                      refine.ts when it passed the 400-line ceiling. No VS Code
                      dependency."
     :exports ["extractFormContent" "buildRefinePrompt" "buildResolveOpenPrompt"
               "buildCritiquePrompt" "buildArguePrompt" "buildRefreshGoalsPrompt"
               "effectiveTotal"]
     :est-lines 280}
    {:module "commands/decide-prompt.ts"
     :responsibility "Pure prompt builder for decideCandidate: a substantive evaluation
                      of one candidate that ends in a forced verdict, recorded as a
                      dated f/reassess. No VS Code dependency; today is a parameter."
     :exports ["buildDecidePrompt" "DecidePromptInput"]
     :est-lines 110}
    {:module "commands/reassess.ts"
     :responsibility "reassessCandidate and hoistCandidate commands: insert an
                      f/reassess history entry or an f/hoist form after the
                      candidate in the editor."
     :exports ["registerReassessCommands" "findFormEndLine"]
     :est-lines 180}
    {:module "providers/candidate-gutter.ts"
     :responsibility "Gutter icon per f/candidate, coloured by its lifecycle status;
                      refreshed on editor change and save."
     :exports ["registerCandidateGutter"]
     :est-lines 120}
    {:module "providers/disk-lint.ts"
     :responsibility "Line decorations on targets whose declared :modules are present
                      or missing on disk; refreshed on editor change and save."
     :exports ["registerDiskLintDecorations"]
     :est-lines 125}])

;;; As with the server, no module nears the limit. The largest (refine-prompts.ts, ~280)
;;; has ample headroom, and the single dist/extension.js is produced by esbuild —
;;; not authored by hand.

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section VII — VSIX enhancement candidates
;;; All cluster :cluster-vsix. Scored on four dimensions, max 40.
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(f/candidate :codelens-candidate-score
  :label   "Score badge + status pill on f/candidate CodeLens"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 8 :elegance 9 :containment 9 :value 8}
  :conditions ["Agree on the lens title format before coding — e.g. 'score: 33 · planned'
                vs. '33 · planned · 1d' — the title is the user-facing contract and
                changing it later is a visible churn for anyone already using the extension"]
  :rationale "Every f/candidate form already gets a ✨ Refine lens from findFormOccurrences,
              but it tells you nothing about the form's weight. The at-a-glance information an
              author needs most is: total score, status, and how recent it is. A CodeLens reading
              'score: 33 · planned · 1d ago' costs one regex to detect the f/candidate keyword,
              one parse of the :scores map (already available via toCnjModel), and one line of
              lens title construction — all pure, no I/O, and entirely inside codelens.ts.
              The score computation is already in foresight.ts (totalScore). No new module needed.")

(f/candidate :codelens-candidate-actions
  :label   "Reassess + Hoist buttons on f/candidate CodeLens"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 6 :elegance 8 :containment 7 :value 9}
  :conditions ["codelens-candidate-score implemented first so the score is visible in
                context when the action button appears"
               "agree on the f/reassess insertion contract: insert a :history vector after
                the existing :scores field in-place vs. append a standalone f/reassess block
                after the closing paren — both are valid EDN but the file diff differs"]
  :rationale "The current workflow to reassess a candidate requires manually editing the
              .cnj file: find the form, update :scores, append a :history vector with the
              f/reassess sub-form, write the rationale, get the indentation right. That is
              friction that compounds with every iteration. A '📊 Reassess' CodeLens button
              above each active f/candidate opens a multi-step VS Code input box (score per
              dimension → rationale text), computes the new total, and inserts the f/reassess
              form via WorkspaceEdit. A conditional '⬆ Hoist' button appears when status is
              :planned and score ≥ 28 — same pattern: dialog collects :into binding + note,
              inserts f/hoist, updates :status to :hoisted. Containment is 7 because the
              command registration touches both a new command handler and codelens.ts.")

(f/candidate :disk-lint-decorations
  :label   "Inline disk-consistency state per target (gutter + hover)"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 7 :elegance 8 :containment 7 :value 8}
  :conditions ["agree on decoration update cadence: on-save with debounce (nicer UX,
                requires onDidSaveTextDocument listener) vs. on-demand via a 'Check disk'
                CodeLens (zero background work, but manual) — cadence drives the module
                shape"
               "lint_disk_consistency stable with suffix matching confirmed on real
                workspace (already done this session)"]
  :rationale "When a .cnj file is open, there is no visual signal about whether the declared
              :modules in each target actually exist on disk — the author must run a tool or
              read the file system manually. TextEditorDecorationType gutter icons give the
              answer at one glance: a green ✓ beside each target block when all modules are
              found, a red ✗ with the missing-count when they are not. Hovering the icon
              shows the missing module names verbatim (sourced from lint_disk_consistency
              output). The check runs via the core bridge (same lint logic, no network hop)
              after each save. A new providers/disk-lint.ts module holds the decoration
              type definitions and the update function; codelens.ts is unchanged.")

(f/candidate :candidate-lifecycle-gutter
  :label   "Lifecycle-status gutter dots per f/candidate line"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 8 :elegance 8 :containment 8 :value 7}
  :conditions ["Decide icon representation before building: VS Code gutter icons require
                either an SVG asset file (gutterIconPath) or a unicode character as a
                decoration letter — agree on which so the right assets or font choices
                are prepared (SVG is sharper; unicode needs no extra files)"]
  :rationale "A .cnj file with fifteen candidates is visually uniform — all the same text
              colour, no lifecycle signal until you read the :status keyword on each form.
              A coloured gutter dot beside the opening line of each f/candidate makes lifecycle
              state scannable in the same way git gutter decorations make change state scannable:
              orange = :proposed, blue = :planned, green = :hoisted, grey = :abandoned.
              f/reassess sub-forms get a small 'trajectory' marker (↑ or ↓ based on score
              delta vs. the base). The entire feature is two TextEditorDecorationType
              registrations in a new providers/candidate-gutter.ts and a pass over the
              parsed model — no command, no I/O, pure decoration.")

(f/candidate :foresight-health-statusbar
  :label   "Workspace foresight-health badge in VS Code status bar"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 9 :elegance 9 :containment 9 :value 7}
  :conditions ["Decide update trigger before building: file-open only (cheapest),
                file-save (responsive), or a workspace FileSystemWatcher (live but adds
                a background path) — the trigger determines whether status-bar.ts needs
                a watcher or just an onDidChangeActiveTextEditor listener"]
  :rationale "lens_foresight_health reports stale candidates, thin rationale, orphaned
              hoists, and high-score/no-conditions gaps — but today those diagnostics only
              surface when you explicitly invoke the MCP tool. A status bar item costs
              three lines to register and one background evaluation on file-open: when any
              .cnj file is active it shows '✓ backlog healthy' (green) or '⚠ 3 foresight
              issues' (amber). Clicking opens a VS Code Quick Pick listing each issue with
              a jump-to-line action. The evaluation runs the same logic as the MCP tool via
              the core bridge — no additional server dependency. Containment is 9 because
              it is self-contained in a single status-bar.ts module and touches nothing else.")

(f/candidate :validate-on-save
  :label   "Auto-validate .cnj on save with squiggle diagnostics"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 8 :elegance 8 :containment 8 :value 8}
  :conditions ["settle debounce strategy: validate on every save vs. only when the file
                content hash has changed since the last validation result — the latter
                avoids redundant work on rapid-fire saves but requires caching the hash"]
  :rationale "The validate command exists but it is manual — the author must invoke it to
              see violations. The design's :open question asks exactly this: live vs. on-demand.
              Resolving it in favour of on-save (debounced, 300 ms) gives the familiar TypeScript
              experience: save, see squiggles, fix, repeat. The implementation is an
              onDidSaveTextDocument listener in commands/validate.ts that calls the existing
              handleValidate logic, maps Violation records to vscode.Diagnostic, and pushes
              them to a DiagnosticCollection. The debounce guard prevents hammering on
              ctrl+s workflows. This also resolves the charter :open question explicitly —
              recording the decision in the charter.")

(f/candidate :charter-ref-lens
  :label   "Reference-health summary in charter CodeLens"
  :cluster :cluster-vsix
  :added   "2026-06-10"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 8 :elegance 8 :containment 8 :value 7}
  :conditions ["Decide display policy for charters with zero :references before coding:
                show '0 references' (informative), show nothing (cleaner), or suppress
                the lens entirely — each choice has different UX implications for
                single-file projects vs. multi-file workspaces"]
  :rationale "The charter CodeLens currently offers three actions but none of them tell you
              at a glance how many of the charter's :references are resolvable. Adding a
              lens reading '3/3 references resolved' (green) or '1 broken reference' (amber)
              costs one existsSync pass over doc.charter.references — work already done in
              handleValidate. When broken, the lens becomes a click-to-open action navigating
              to the offending reference path. This gives the author the same at-a-glance
              signal that lint_disk_consistency provides for modules, but surfaced directly
              in the charter CodeLens row without a separate tool invocation.")

